  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThis Weekend: Gradual Warming Trend, Dry \n\nCold air advection continues for one more day as surface high \npressure sinks out of Canada and into the Upper Midwest. This will \nkeep temperatures on the chilly side again in the upper 30s to low \n40s. As we head into the weekend, upper-level ridging and the \naforementioned surface high building in will allow for some very \nweak southerly return flow. Consequently, this should allow \ntemperatures to slowly begin on a warming trend with highs returning \nto the 40s for much of the area for the weekend.\n\nA few snow flurries will remain possible through midday today for \nportions of North Central Wisconsin given slight low level \ninstability and steep low level lapse rates remaining in place. Low \nlevel moisture below 800 mb remains hard to come by but a shortwave \nperturbation interacting with some of the mid-level moisture fetch \nfrom Lake Superior remains enough of a possibility that have kept \nflurries in the forecast through midday before the upper trough \nmoves off to the east. After this, with the high pressure moving \ninto place, the forecast is expected to remain dry with the LREF \ngrand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) showing a 0% chance for \nany measurable precipitation this weekend. \n\nNext Week: Mild, Dry Start to the Week with Precip Chances Returning \nLate Week\n\nAs we move into next week, upper level ridging will linger overhead \nfor the first half of the week with the surface high pressure \nremaining predominantly just south to southeast of the region. This \nwill aid in a reinvigorated southerly flow into the local area and \nthus continue the warming trend through the middle of next week. \nHighs by Tuesday should return to the 50s areawide with low-end \nprobabilities (10-40%) for highs to meet or exceed 60 degrees \nWednesday through Friday via NBM guidance. \n\nIn addition to the warmer temperatures, both deterministic and \nensemble guidance hint at the upper ridge breaking down by the mid \nweek time frame and a upper level trough pivoting across the Great \nPlains and into the Midwest by Thursday. With better ensemble \nagreement in this trough, PoPs have started to increase for the late \nweek timeframe (40-60% by Thursday afternoon). Given that \ntemperatures are expected to be well above normal, this should be an \nall rain situation for our local area. \n\nAs we head past next week, the ensemble mean in the GEFS/EC ensemble \nwould suggest some cooler temperatures heading into the week of \nThanksgiving in the wake of the aforementioned upper-level trough, \nhowever confidence in any details remains low at this time.",
